Introduction
An Expert Advisor that trades a funded or challenge account works under two rulebooks at once. The first one is its own strategy logic. The second one belongs to the proprietary trading firm, and the EA usually knows nothing about it. Break the first rulebook and you lose a trade. Break the second one and you lose the account, usually while the terminal is doing exactly what you told it to do.
MetaTrader 5 offers nothing for that second rulebook. There is no account-level constraint object, no prop-day calendar, and no notion of a challenge that started with a fixed initial balance. The daily limit resets at an hour the broker's clock does not match. The overall floor may sit still, or it may ratchet up behind the account and then freeze there for good.
In practice, the guard is written by hand and converges on a common shape: input thresholds, an OnTick() if-block, a close-all helper, and one per-protection boolean to prevent re-firing. That shape is where the interesting part of this article lives, because it fails in specific ways that stay invisible while the account is healthy.
These failures are not arithmetic; they are structural. They occur when rules observe each other's state, when a value has multiple writers, or when a request and its confirmation share the same code path. Fixing them one comparison at a time produces a guard that fails in a new way the following month.
C1: a threshold defaults to 0.0 and the test is greater-or-equal, so enabling a protection without typing a number satisfies it on the very first tick.
C2: a reset of the monitoring flags sits inside the trigger loop and clears the flags of triggers that the same pass has not evaluated yet.
C3: the Algo Trading toggle is fire-and-forget, so a cascade can switch trading off and then straight back on within one pass.
C4: the overall drawdown recomputes its reference from the current balance whenever it fires, so the floor walks down with the account.
C5: the daily rollover never rearms the intraday flags, so one firing disarms the daily protection for the rest of the run.
C6: an excursion measurement reuses the drop-from-peak formula and routes the result to a profit action, so a pure loss triggers a profit response.
C7: the check runs immediately after an asynchronous action, reads the value from before it, and concludes the action worked.
C8: the close-all routine ignores every retcode and reports the account protected, so a requote or a closed market leaves positions open.
C9: nothing is persisted, so a reboot of the virtual server refills the consumed drawdown budget.
Three of those nine (C3, C7, and C8) belong to the acting half and are built in Part 2. The other six are structural; where a fix here also needs the persisted state or the facade from Part 2, the text says so.
Prop-firm rule guard — architecture
There are 23 headers under Include/PropRulebook/, grouped by role and read top to bottom in dependency order. Arrows show the aggregate flow, not every include.
Much of this ground has been walked on this site already, and the reader may recognize it from earlier articles. The three articles below overlap with the observation layer directly, and each one solves a narrower version of the same problem. None of them assembles the whole set of firm rules into a single frozen view of the account.
Implementing a Daily Loss Limit and Drawdown Circuit Breaker in MQL5
computes the daily result on every tick against broker midnight, closes the positions on breach, and holds a halted flag until the next rollover.
Creating a Profit Concentration Analyzer in MQL5
works out the arithmetic the consistency rule rests on, which is the largest single day measured against total profit.
Building a Unified Discipline Framework in MQL5
folds a symbol whitelist, a session and news filter, and a daily trade counter into one discipline engine shared by an indicator and an EA.
What this piece adds is the account arithmetic those leave out, plus the idea that a finding and an action are two different things. The record every rule reads here is a plain struct, filled once per pass and passed to every rule as a const reference. The type is not immutable, but no rule can change it during a pass, and that is what makes a pass reproducible.
This is the first of two articles. Part 1 covers the observation layer (records, a rule contract, and snapshot builders). Part 2 covers the decision layer (rules, two-phase resolution, actuators, persisted state, and the facade).
What the rulebook actually asks for
Before any class exists, the requirements have to be pinned down, and prop-firm rules are less uniform than they first look. Three distinctions shape the observation layer directly. Each one changes what has to be measured rather than what has to be compared, so each one lands in a record or in a clock instead of landing in a rule.
Maximum drawdown comes in several mechanics
. The static variant is a fixed amount below the starting balance and never moves for the life of the account. The trailing variant starts at the same distance but ratchets up with the account peak and never descends, either on intraday equity or on the end-of-day balance, and some programs freeze that floor at the starting balance. Hybrid rules exist too. Guarding a trailing account with static calculations is not a rounding error. It is the wrong floor entirely.
The daily reset hour is not midnight
. It varies per firm, and it is expressed in the firm's own time zone rather than the broker server's. A loss booked at 16:55 server time and one booked at 17:05 may or may not belong to the same prop day, depending entirely on two numbers the trader has to supply.
Some rules block a payout, not the account
. The consistency rule divides the best single day's profit by total profit, and a violation withholds the withdrawal until the distribution rebalances. The same holds for the minimum trading days requirement. A guard that answers either of those by closing positions is actively harming the trader.
Freezing the account into one record
The first design decision stops one rule from changing what the next one reads, and everything else depends on it. If a rule reads live account state, then a rule that fires and closes positions changes what the next rule reads. Evaluate eight rules in a loop, let the third one flatten the account, and rules four through eight look at a different account than rules one through three. This does not fix C2 by itself: C2 is a reset inside the trigger loop, and the order of the pass closes it by rearming every rule before any of them is evaluated.
So rules do not read the account at all. They read a record that is built once per pass and handed to every rule as a const reference. The file Include/PropRulebook/PropAccountSnapshot.mqh declares it. Apart from Clear(), the struct has no method that changes anything, no threshold, and no comparison. It is a value type, cleared and refilled once per pass. Its fields are public, so read-only comes from the const reference, not from the type.
struct
PropAccountSnapshot
  {
//--- pass identity
ulong
pass_id;
// monotonic counter, 1 for the first pass
datetime
server_time;
// TimeCurrent() at the moment of the build
//--- account figures (each terminal function called exactly once)
double
balance;
double
equity;
double
credit;
double
floating_pl;
// equity - balance - credit
double
margin;
double
free_margin;
double
margin_level;
// percent, 0.0 when margin is 0
double
initial_balance;
// challenge anchor supplied by the facade
//--- prop-firm calendar (from CPropSessionClock)
int
prop_day_index;
// integer day number under the firm's reset hour
datetime
prop_day_start;
// server time of the current prop day's boundary
bool
is_new_prop_day;
// true only on the first pass of a new prop day
bool
past_weekend_cutoff;
// pre-weekend flat cutoff already reached
int
minutes_to_weekend_cutoff;
// ...
};
The record carries five more groups: the opening balance and realized result of the current prop day, the ledger aggregates the payout rules need, the excursion measurements, the exposure figures, and the calendar proximity fields. Everything a rule is allowed to know is in there, and all of it is read in one pass before the first rule runs. That is a sequence of AccountInfoDouble() calls and one sweep of the positions, not an atomic read, but every rule sees the same values.
//--- convenience readers used by several rules
bool
HasExposure()
const
{
return
positions_total >
0
; }
double
DayLoss()
const
{
return
day_start_balance - equity; }
// positive while losing
//--- the mirror of DayLoss: positive while the prop day is up
double
DayProfit()
const
{
return
equity - day_start_balance; }
//--- profit since the challenge started, measured from the fixed anchor
double
ChallengeProfit()
const
{
return
equity - initial_balance; }
Note the field initial_balance. The snapshot does not capture the challenge anchor; it receives it. The contract around the record is enforced by convention rather than by the compiler, so a rule must never call the account or the calendar API on its own. The verification script asserts that every verdict of a pass carries the same timestamp, which shows the reports are consistent, not that a rule stayed away from the terminal.
The second decision is the other half of the same fix. If a rule can act, then evaluating rules and taking action are the same phase, and phase separation is impossible. So a rule returns a verdict and nothing else. The file Include/PropRulebook/PropRuleVerdict.mqh defines three enumerations before the record itself, and two of them are deliberately independent axes.
enum
ENUM_PROP_SEVERITY
  {
   PROP_SEV_OK       =
0
,
// within limits
PROP_SEV_INFO     =
1
,
// informational progress report
PROP_SEV_WARNING  =
2
,
// inside the safety buffer, not breached
PROP_SEV_ADVISORY =
3
,
// a payout-blocking condition, account is safe
PROP_SEV_BREACH   =
4
// the firm's limit itself is violated
};
enum
ENUM_PROP_DIRECTIVE
  {
   PROP_DIRECTIVE_NONE              =
0
,
// nothing to do
PROP_DIRECTIVE_ADVISORY          =
1
,
// log/notify only, never touches the account
PROP_DIRECTIVE_BLOCK_NEW_ENTRIES =
2
,
// CanOpen() must return false
PROP_DIRECTIVE_FLATTEN           =
3
,
// close relevant exposure, keep trading enabled
PROP_DIRECTIVE_LOCKDOWN          =
4
// close exposure AND disable Algo Trading
};
Keeping severity and directive apart is what lets the consistency rule report PROP_SEV_ADVISORY, because something is genuinely wrong, while asking for PROP_DIRECTIVE_ADVISORY, which touches nothing at all. With a single axis, reporting a payout rule as serious means picking a value that also says act hard, and only extra mapping keeps it from closing positions. Two axes do not guarantee the right action either: the coordinator in Part 2 has to act on the directive alone.
struct
PropRuleVerdict
  {
   ENUM_PROP_RULE      rule;
bool
evaluated;
// false when the rule is not registered at all
bool
abstained;
// rule registered but had no usable input this pass
bool
latched;
// rule is holding a breach latch from an earlier pass
ENUM_PROP_SEVERITY  severity;
   ENUM_PROP_DIRECTIVE directive;
double
measured;
// what the rule observed
double
threshold;
// the limit it was compared against
double
headroom;
// distance still available before breach
double
projected;
// forward-looking figure, rule specific (see CConsistencyRule)
string
unit;
// "ccy", "share", "days", "minutes"
string
message;
datetime
stamp;
// ...
};
The fields evaluated and abstained are worth separating. A rule that was never registered reports the first one; a rule that ran with nothing usable to work with reports the second. Both differ from a clean pass, and collapsing them into one boolean is how a guard gives a false all-clear while its data source is down.
The third record is the configuration, and C1 dies here in a single sentence: enablement is a flag and never a side effect of a value. The file Include/PropRulebook/PropFirmProfile.mqh pairs every threshold with its own bool. A profile straight out of Clear() has every flag false and every threshold zero, and it registers exactly zero rules.
//--- static maximum drawdown (C4)
bool
use_static_dd;
double
static_dd_amount;
// account currency, measured from initial_balance
//--- trailing maximum drawdown
bool
use_trailing_dd;
double
trailing_dd_amount;
// account currency below the running peak
ENUM_PROP_TRAIL_MODE trailing_mode;
bool
trailing_lock_enabled;
// freeze the floor once the lock level is reached
double
trailing_lock_level;
// balance level at which the floor freezes;
// 0 means initial_balance + trailing_dd_amount
A flag alone is not sufficient, though. Someone will tick the static drawdown switch and leave the amount at zero. Validate() catches that before the first tick and names the field that has to be fixed. A disabled rule with a zero threshold passes without comment, because that zero is legal precisely when nothing will ever read it.
if
(use_static_dd && static_dd_amount <=
0.0
)
        { err =
"static_dd_amount is enabled but unset (0.0)"
;
return
false
; }
if
(use_trailing_dd && trailing_dd_amount <=
0.0
)
        { err =
"trailing_dd_amount is enabled but unset (0.0)"
;
return
false
; }
if
(use_daily_loss && daily_loss_amount <=
0.0
)
        { err =
"daily_loss_amount is enabled but unset (0.0)"
;
return
false
; }
if
(use_consistency && (consistency_max_share <=
0.0
|| consistency_max_share >=
1.0
))
        { err =
"consistency_max_share is enabled but not a share in (0,1)"
;
return
false
; }
One contract for every rule
Eight evaluators with eight ad-hoc signatures is eight places to get the life cycle wrong. The file Include/PropRulebook/IPropRule.mqh reduces them to four operations plus serialization, and the two reset operations being separate is the fix for C5. Nothing outside the registry ever knows which concrete rule type it is holding.
//--- Reads the rule's own slice of the profile. MUST return false
//--- (with a human-readable err) when the rule is switched on but
//--- its threshold is unset -- never "fall back to a default", the
//--- silent-default habit is exactly what caused C1.
virtual
bool
Configure(
const
PropFirmProfile &profile,
string
&err) =
0
;
//--- One pass. Read snap; write out and the rule's own fields.
//--- Forbidden: calling any terminal state function, touching
//--- another rule, triggering any reset.
virtual
void
Evaluate(
const
PropAccountSnapshot &snap, PropRuleVerdict &out) =
0
;
//--- Full challenge reset. Clears account-level latches and
//--- re-anchors whatever the rule anchors. Reachable only from
//--- CPropRulebook::ResetChallenge() and the restore-rollback
//--- branch inside Init() -- never from a routine trigger.
virtual
void
Reset(
const
PropAccountSnapshot &snap) =
0
;
//--- Prop-day boundary. Rearms intraday latches and rebases
//--- per-day baselines. MUST NOT clear an account-level latch and
//--- MUST NOT move a challenge anchor or a trailing lock (C4/C5).
virtual
void
ResetIntraday(
const
PropAccountSnapshot &snap) =
0
;
Reset() means a brand new challenge: clear the account-level latches and re-anchor. ResetIntraday() means a new prop day: rearm the intraday latches, rebase the per-day baselines, and touch nothing that belongs to the challenge. A single combined routine has to pick one of those meanings, and either pick is wrong half the time.
The base class carries the bookkeeping every concrete rule would otherwise duplicate. Report() fills the identity, the stamp, and the unit fields in one place, and Abstain() is its sibling for the registered-but-blind case. One shared helper then gives every rule the same early warning without each one inventing its own.
//--- helper every concrete rule uses to fill its verdict, so the
//--- unit/rule/stamp bookkeeping is written in exactly one place
void
Report(PropRuleVerdict &out,
const
PropAccountSnapshot &snap,
const
ENUM_PROP_SEVERITY severity,
const
ENUM_PROP_DIRECTIVE directive,
const
double
measured,
const
double
threshold,
const
double
headroom,
const
string
unit,
const
string
message,
const
bool
latched =
false
)
     {
      out.Clear();
      out.rule      = m_id;
      out.evaluated =
true
;
      out.abstained =
false
;
      out.latched   = latched;
      out.severity  = severity;
      out.directive = directive;
      out.measured  = measured;
      out.threshold = threshold;
      out.headroom  = headroom;
      out.unit      = unit;
      out.message   = message;
      out.stamp     = snap.server_time;
      m_last        = out;
     }
//--- WARNING once the consumed share of a limit passes the buffer
ENUM_PROP_SEVERITY BufferSeverity(
const
double
consumed,
const
double
limit)
const
{
if
(limit <=
0.0
)
return
PROP_SEV_OK;
if
(consumed >= limit * m_buffer_ratio)
return
PROP_SEV_WARNING;
return
PROP_SEV_OK;
     }
With the safety buffer ratio at its default of 0.80, a rule starts warning once four-fifths of its allowance is gone. Serialization is part of the contract rather than an afterthought, because C9 is not fixable by bolting persistence onto the outside of a rule table. Each rule knows which part of its own state matters.
//--- Restart survival (C9). Serialize() returns a single line with
//--- no '=' and no newline; Deserialize() must reject anything it
//--- does not fully understand by returning false, so the store can
//--- fall back to a fresh anchor instead of loading partial data.
virtual
string
Serialize()
const
{
return
""
; }
//--- the default: accept an empty payload and keep the current state
virtual
bool
Deserialize(
const
string
state) {
return
(
StringLen
(state) ==
0
); }
Where a day begins and what it earned
Include/PropRulebook/CPropSessionClock.mqh is the only component that maps timestamps to calendar boundaries. Everything else receives the answer precomputed in the snapshot. The time model is worth stating explicitly, because getting the sign of the offset wrong silently misattributes a whole day of profit and loss.
firm_local_time = server_time + timezone_offset_hours *
3600
The reset hour is expressed in firm-local time, so the boundary lands on the server hour that is the reset hour minus the offset. A reset at 15:00 with an offset of minus 2 puts the boundary at 17:00 server time. The conversion below is the whole of it, and every other date question in the package reduces to this one.
//--- Server time at which the prop day containing t began.
datetime
PropDayStart(
const
datetime
t)
const
{
long
offset_secs = (
long
)m_offset_hours *
3600
;
long
local       = (
long
)t + offset_secs;
MqlDateTime
lt;
TimeToStruct
((
datetime
)local, lt);
long
sod        = (
long
)lt.hour *
3600
+ (
long
)lt.min *
60
+ (
long
)lt.sec;
long
midnight   = local - sod;
long
boundary   = midnight + (
long
)m_reset_hour *
3600
+ (
long
)m_reset_minute *
60
;
if
(local < boundary)
         boundary -=
86400
;
return
(
datetime
)(boundary - offset_secs);
     }
//--- Stateful rollover detector. Returns true exactly once per prop
//--- day: on the first call whose timestamp belongs to a day index
//--- different from the last one seen. A clock with no day index
//--- yet only records it and returns false -- starting the EA is
//--- not a rollover. A restored index is compared like any other,
//--- so a restart on a later prop day rolls over exactly once.
bool
DetectRollover(
const
datetime
t)
     {
int
idx = PropDayIndex(t);
if
(!m_has_day_index)
        {
         m_last_day_index = idx;
         m_has_day_index  =
true
;
return
false
;
        }
if
(idx == m_last_day_index)
return
false
;

      m_last_day_index = idx;
return
true
;
     }
The first call after configuration returns false. Starting the EA is not a rollover, and if it were treated as one, every terminal restart would rearm the intraday latches the guard had just consumed. That is why the day index belongs in the serialized state: a machine that reboots at 03:00 must not treat 03:01 as a new prop day. A restored index also works the other way: if the terminal was off across the rollover, the first pass after the restart returns true once and the daily protections rearm.
The weekend cutoff is deliberately not converted through the offset. A broker's Friday close is a server-clock event, so the cutoff day, hour, and minute are read as plain server time and compared through a seconds-of-week key. Midnight is a legal cutoff hour, so zero cannot mean unconfigured, and the profile supplies a sentinel instead.
//--- Pre-weekend flat cutoff, in server time.
bool
IsPastWeekendCutoff(
const
datetime
t)
const
{
if
(!m_use_weekend_cutoff || m_cut_hour == PROP_UNSET_HOUR)
return
false
;
return
(WeekKey(t) >= CutoffKey());
     }
The consistency rule needs to know which single prop day produced the most profit. The minimum-trading-days rule needs to know how many days carried a qualifying trade. Neither question can be answered from the account's current state, because both of them need history, and that history has to follow the firm's calendar rather than the server's.
The file Include/PropRulebook/CDailyProfitLedger.mqh keeps one record per day in a bounded array and resolves a timestamp to a record through the clock. Deals arrive one at a time, and the aggregation per day is deliberately small: a realized sum, a volume sum, a deal count, and the largest single volume seen on that day.
//--- Books one executed deal. net_profit already includes swap and
//--- commission; volume is the deal volume in lots and is used for
//--- the qualifying-day test only (an entry deal carries volume but
//--- no profit, an exit deal carries both).
bool
RecordDeal(
const
datetime
deal_time,
const
double
net_profit,
const
double
volume)
     {
if
(!m_ready)
return
false
;
int
pos = Resolve(deal_time);
if
(pos <
0
)
return
false
;

      m_days[pos].realized     += net_profit;
      m_days[pos].total_volume += volume;
      m_days[pos].deal_count++;
if
(volume > m_days[pos].largest_volume)
         m_days[pos].largest_volume = volume;
return
true
;
     }
The field largest_volume rather than total_volume is what the qualifying-day test reads, and the two are not interchangeable. The rule as modeled here counts a day only when one trade reaches the minimum size, so four trades of 0.005 lots do not make one qualifying 0.01-lot trade. Firms that count differently need a different test.
//--- Days carrying at least one trade of min_volume lots or more.
//--- A day with only a 0.005-lot trade against a 0.01 minimum does
//--- not count, which is exactly how the firms count it.
int
QualifyingDays(
const
double
min_volume)
const
{
int
n =
0
;
for
(
int
i =
0
; i <
ArraySize
(m_days); i++)
if
(m_days[i].largest_volume >= min_volume)
            n++;
return
n;
     }
The best-day share has an arithmetic trap in it. With a negative total, the statement that the best day is 300% of total profit is a true division and a meaningless answer. So the ledger returns zero, and the consistency rule never reads it as a pass: with no positive realized profit, it abstains.
//--- best single day / total profit, as a 0..1 share. Returns 0
//--- when total profit is not positive, because "the best day is
//--- 300% of a negative total" is arithmetic, not a rule breach.
double
BestDayShare()
const
{
double
total = TotalRealized();
if
(total <=
0.0
)
return
0.0
;
return
BestDayProfit() / total;
     }
The forward-looking method is the interesting one, and it exists for a reason that only becomes clear in Part 2. It answers what the share would become if a given profit were realized right now, on the day that contains a given timestamp. The snapshot builder calls it with the current floating result, and what that buys is a guard able to refuse a profit which would break a payout rule.
//--- P5. The share the account WOULD show if extra_profit were
//--- realized on the prop day containing 'when' right now.
//--- CPropSnapshotBuilder calls this with the current floating P/L
//--- while assembling the frozen snapshot; the coordinator only
//--- reads the resulting verdict before allowing a profit-target
//--- flatten.
double
ProjectedBestDayShare(
const
datetime
when,
const
double
extra_profit)
const
{
if
(m_clock ==
NULL
)
return
0.0
;
int
today_idx = m_clock.PropDayIndex(when);
int
pos       = Find(today_idx);
double
total =
0.0
;
double
best  =
0.0
;
for
(
int
i =
0
; i <
ArraySize
(m_days); i++)
        {
double
value = m_days[i].realized;
if
(i == pos)
            value += extra_profit;
         total += value;
if
(value > best)
            best = value;
        }
//--- today has no record yet: the projected day is a new one
if
(pos <
0
)
        {
         total += extra_profit;
if
(extra_profit > best)
            best = extra_profit;
        }
if
(total <=
0.0
)
return
0.0
;
return
best / total;
     }
Measuring without judging
The excursion tracker is the structural answer to C6, and it is the shortest class in the package that carries the most design intent. Maximum favorable and maximum adverse excursion are useful numbers. They are also, arithmetically, drop-from-peak and rise-from-trough, which are the same two formulas a drawdown rule uses.
The failure mode was never a wrong formula. It was a correct formula wired to the wrong action, and labeling a peak drop as profit is only possible when something is in a position to label it at all. So the file Include/PropRulebook/CExcursionTracker.mqh does not derive from the rule interface. It has no threshold, no enable flag, and no verdict.
//--- called once per pass by the snapshot builder, with the same
//--- balance/equity pair every rule of that pass will read
void
Update(
const
double
balance,
const
double
equity)
     {
if
(!m_seeded)
return
;
if
(balance > m_peak_balance)
         m_peak_balance   = balance;
if
(balance < m_trough_balance)
         m_trough_balance = balance;
if
(equity  > m_peak_equity)
         m_peak_equity    = equity;
if
(equity  < m_trough_equity)
         m_trough_equity  = equity;
     }
//--- MFE: the best the account ever was, above the anchor. Never negative.
double
MfeBalance()
const
{
return
MathMax
(
0.0
, m_peak_balance - m_anchor); }
//--- best excursion above the anchor, clamped so it never reads negative
double
MfeEquity()
const
{
return
MathMax
(
0.0
, m_peak_equity  - m_anchor); }
//--- MAE: the worst the account ever was, below the anchor, reported
//--- as a positive magnitude. Never negative.
double
MaeBalance()
const
{
return
MathMax
(
0.0
, m_anchor - m_trough_balance); }
//--- worst excursion below the anchor, clamped the same way
double
MaeEquity()
const
{
return
MathMax
(
0.0
, m_anchor - m_trough_equity); }
The clamping at zero is the last mile of the same idea. On an account that only ever lost money, the favorable excursion is zero rather than a negative number that some comparison might read as a profit. When a bug comes from two concepts sharing a formula, the durable fix is to leave one of them no way to reach a decision.
MQL5 ships a native economic calendar, and it is unavailable more often than you would like. A feed that returns no events on failure silently switches the news protection off, and a feed that assumes an event blocks every entry forever. So the file Include/PropRulebook/CEconomicCalendarFeed.mqh carries a third state, and it probes for that state exactly once.
