Limitations
No tick, no pass
. The snapshot is built when the consuming EA asks for it. Over a weekend, during a broker outage, or on a symbol that stops ticking, nothing is observed and therefore nothing is enforced. A gap that opens beyond the floor is already beyond the floor when the first tick after it finally arrives. So the weekend cutoff, the news blackout, and the drawdown floors are not independent loops; they run only when the EA runs a pass.
The ledger only knows what it was told
. Deals reach it through the trade transaction handler or through a direct injection call. There is no history backfill at startup, so deals executed while the terminal was closed, or before the guard was ever attached, stay invisible to the consistency share and to the qualifying-day count.
The day baseline is captured at the first pass of the day, not at the boundary
. If the terminal is offline when the prop day rolls over, the opening balance is captured whenever the first pass after reconnection runs. Any result realized in between is attributed to the new day's opening balance rather than measured as part of the day's loss.
The native calendar does not work in the Strategy Tester, and the ledger is a bounded ring
. A backtest therefore exercises the feed's unavailable path rather than its data path. The ring defaults to 120 prop days, so a longer challenge computes its consistency share and its qualifying-day count over a moving window rather than over the full history.
Several firm and broker policies are fixed in this model
. They are known limits, not settings:
The time zone offset is in whole hours: no daylight saving, no half-hour or 45-minute zones, and no broker offset that moves on its own.
The daily loss is day_start_balance - equity. Measuring it from starting equity, closed results, or the intraday equity low needs another helper.
Credit is always excluded (floating_pl = equity - balance - credit), with no setting to count it.
Drawdown covers a static floor on equity and a trailing floor. A static floor on the closed balance, or a hybrid rule, needs its own rule class.
A qualifying day needs one trade of the minimum size. Counting total volume, trade count, or holding time needs another test.
Synthetic mode cannot model a calendar that is available but empty; the closest test puts one event outside the blackout window.
Nothing in the language stops a rule from calling the terminal or calendar API, so a new rule can break the contract without a compile error.
Conclusion
Part 1 removed several failure modes by changing shape rather than by adding checks. Freeze the account into one record per pass and rules can no longer disturb each other. Make a rule report a verdict instead of taking an action and evaluation separates cleanly from resolution. Pair every threshold with its own flag and reject any profile that fails Validate(), and an unconfigured protection cannot fire.
What exists at this point is an observation layer: three records, one rule contract, and five classes that resolve a session boundary, a per-day ledger, an excursion measurement, a calendar that admits when it is blind, and one builder that writes the snapshot. Part 2 turns that observation into a decision, and then into an action that verifies itself.
The files below are attached individually and also together in MQL5.zip, which already carries the folder structure the terminal expects. The package is the complete library, so it also holds the concrete rules and the decision, action, persistence, and facade files (CRuleCoordinator, CProtectiveActionExecutor, CTerminalAlgoSwitch, CGuardStateStore, CPropRulebook), attached so that the demo and the test compile. Part 2 covers them.
